module std.trait_derive_shape

import std.dissolution { DissolutionCondition, unbound_dissolution }

import std.algebra { Cons, Empty }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data trait_derive_shape_note: String = "Target-AGNOSTIC half of the capability model: the ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape source-shape vocabulary and the GENERIC table lookup and completeness predicate, PARAMETERIZED ON THE CAPABILITY CARRIER. THE ROWS ARE NOT HERE and NEITHER IS THE CARRIER. B1 (operator ruling 2026-08-19) replaced the ReprGroundingDeriveTrait coproduct -- sixteen Rust and serde trait names sitting in a std interface -- and moved every Rust-specific row into extdeps.languages.rust.capabilities, so a new target is ROWS in its own extdeps module and never an edit to this fold (DESIGN section 4 N-times-M). WHY THE CARRIER IS A TYPE PARAMETER AND NOT A TYPE, which is the part that was learned the expensive way: an intermediate revision of this migration declared TargetCapabilityKey HERE as Symbol where brand(...), which forced import v2.std.node into this module. required_regen_host compiles stage0 from regen_input_sources, whose roots are HARDCODED to src/v1 and dag (cli_run.rs regen_source_roots) -- src/v2 is not a regen root and never will be, because stage0 IS the v1 seed and a seed that reached into src/v2 would depend on the successor it bootstraps toward. This module is inside that closure: src/v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit and src/v1.compiler.emit_rust both import it. So the import made a closure member depend on a module the closure cannot contain, and required-regen refused BEFORE comparing any bytes. THE INVARIANT, stated so the next person does not rediscover it: A MODULE REACHABLE BY IMPORT FROM src/v1 CANNOT IMPORT v2 ANYTHING. It is closure membership, not seed-projection -- 591 dag/ modules import v2.* quite legally because src/v1 does not reach them. A seed-visible carrier would have satisfied the gate by putting the target-model concept back inside the seed closure, which is the coupling that made the predecessor work by accident; a TYPE PARAMETER removes the dependency instead of relocating it, so this module cannot violate the boundary by construction. There is also no retype target: zero type Symbol declarations exist under src/v1 or dag. Consumed by v2.compiler.trait_derive_completeness at K = TargetCapabilityKey and by v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit at K = RustCapability. NOTHING CAUGHT THIS EXCEPT THE GATE: zero blocking errors on every entry under three source roots, and 9687 of 9687 witnesses green with failed=0 on a self-provenanced floor run. Entry scope is not gate scope, a green floor is not a green regen, and a green regen is not a green corpus -- the three src/v2 consumers of these signatures are outside the regen closure and must be compiled separately."

data trait_derive_shape_grounding_lane_handoff: String = "Root-4 arm (b): coproduct-native arithmetic + PartialOrd/PartialEq impl blocks for algebra carriers (GroupCompletion<Nat>, Vector). Capability table + repr_grounding_derive_completeness_predicate remain the shared authority; v1.compiler.trait_derive_emit emits impl { … } from shape rows (not #[derive]) through the seed emitter. GroupCompletion<M> pos/neg pair Add/Sub/Mul/Div landed in #7197 (dissolves interim CommutativeSemiring<Magnitude> stubs)."

type TargetCapabilityShapeRow<K> {
  shape: ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape
  capabilities: List<K>
}

type TargetCapabilityShapeTable<K> {
  rows: List<TargetCapabilityShapeRow<K>>
}

type ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape
  = ReprDeriveElemKernelString
  | ReprDeriveElemKernelInt
  | ReprDeriveElemKernelBool
  | ReprDeriveElemKernelUnit
  | ReprDeriveElemNullaryEnumCopy
  | ReprDeriveElemSymbolWrappedOrdCarrier
  | ReprDeriveElemPayloadCoproduct
  | ReprDeriveElemUnknown


fn repr_grounding_derive_shape_has_trait<K>(
  table: TargetCapabilityShapeTable<K>,
  shape: ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape,
  capability_key: K
) -> Bool {
  table.rows
    |> any(row => row.shape == shape
                  && (row.capabilities |> any(k => k == capability_key)))
}

fn repr_grounding_derive_completeness_predicate<K>(
  table: TargetCapabilityShapeTable<K>,
  required: List<K>,
  shape: ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape
) -> Bool {
  required |> all(k => repr_grounding_derive_shape_has_trait(
    table: table,
    shape: shape,
    capability_key: k
  ))
}

data pair_completion_shape_note: String = "Target-agnostic authority for the Grothendieck pair-completion operator BODIES on GroupCompletion<M> (std.algebra). Each output component is a polynomial over the four operand components self.pos, self.neg, rhs.pos, rhs.neg: an arm is a sum of terms, a term is a product of factors, a factor names one operand component. Selection (which traits apply) stays in the capability table above; SPELLING (trait paths, where-clauses, field syntax) lives per target in extdeps.languages.rust.emit. PairCompletionOp IS A DELIBERATE MINT AND THE PREDECESSOR NOTE DECLINED TO MAKE IT, so the reversal is recorded rather than left as drift: that note said it reused ReprGroundingDeriveTrait as the op key RATHER THAN MINTING A PARALLEL OPERATOR ENUM, which was right while one key set was visible to both this module and its consumers. It no longer is -- the capability carrier is now a type parameter here and a branded Symbol in v2, and neither can be named from inside the src/v1 regen closure that contains this module. The alternative was to parameterize these rows too, which is impossible: a generic K cannot be CONSTRUCTED, and pair_completion_op_rows constructs its keys. So the choice was mint or move the Grothendieck arithmetic into a target module, and moving it would be the section 3 violation. What is minted is also genuinely smaller and different: five algebraic operators, not sixteen target capabilities, and the mapping between them is the target's business -- extdeps.languages.rust.emit joins on this operator identity."

type PairCompletionOperand
  = PairCompletionSelf
  | PairCompletionRhs

type PairCompletionComponent
  = PairCompletionPos
  | PairCompletionNeg

type PairCompletionFactor {
  operand: PairCompletionOperand
  component: PairCompletionComponent
}

type PairCompletionTerm {
  factors: List<PairCompletionFactor>
}

type PairCompletionArm {
  terms: List<PairCompletionTerm>
}

type PairCompletionBody
  = PairCompletionSumOfProducts { pos: PairCompletionArm, neg: PairCompletionArm }
  | PairCompletionNegatedAddend
  | PairCompletionCanonicalQuotient

type PairCompletionOp
  = PairCompletionOpNeg
  | PairCompletionOpAdd
  | PairCompletionOpSub
  | PairCompletionOpMul
  | PairCompletionOpDiv

type PairCompletionOpRow {
  op: PairCompletionOp
  body: PairCompletionBody
}

fn pair_completion_factor(
  operand: PairCompletionOperand,
  component: PairCompletionComponent
) -> PairCompletionFactor {
  PairCompletionFactor { operand: operand, component: component }
}

fn pair_completion_op_rows() -> List<PairCompletionOpRow> {
  [
    PairCompletionOpRow {
      op: PairCompletionOpNeg,
      body: PairCompletionSumOfProducts {
        pos: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionNeg)]
            }
          ]
        },
        neg: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionPos)]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    PairCompletionOpRow {
      op: PairCompletionOpAdd,
      body: PairCompletionSumOfProducts {
        pos: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionPos)]
            },
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionPos)]
            }
          ]
        },
        neg: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionNeg)]
            },
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionNeg)]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    PairCompletionOpRow { op: PairCompletionOpSub, body: PairCompletionNegatedAddend },
    PairCompletionOpRow {
      op: PairCompletionOpMul,
      body: PairCompletionSumOfProducts {
        pos: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionPos),
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionPos)
              ]
            },
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionNeg),
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionNeg)
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        neg: PairCompletionArm {
          terms: [
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionPos),
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionNeg)
              ]
            },
            PairCompletionTerm {
              factors: [
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionSelf, PairCompletionNeg),
                pair_completion_factor(PairCompletionRhs, PairCompletionPos)
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    PairCompletionOpRow { op: PairCompletionOpDiv, body: PairCompletionCanonicalQuotient }
  ]
}

fn pair_completion_arm_uses_rhs(arm: PairCompletionArm) -> Bool {
  arm.terms |> any(term => term.factors |> any(f => match f.operand { PairCompletionRhs => true  PairCompletionSelf => false }))
}

fn pair_completion_body_uses_rhs(body: PairCompletionBody) -> Bool {
  match body {
    PairCompletionSumOfProducts { pos: pos, neg: neg } =>
      pair_completion_arm_uses_rhs(pos) || pair_completion_arm_uses_rhs(neg)
    PairCompletionNegatedAddend => true
    PairCompletionCanonicalQuotient => true
  }
}

data repr_grounding_group_completion_carrier_note: String = "Interim carrier identity for capability-table selection of GroupCompletion pair-completion bodies (#7272 A1 / #7286 handoff). The struct trait surface is selected once via v1_emit_struct_from_capability_table (#[derive] attrs + KernelInt arithmetic impl bodies); the old v1_emit_struct_supplemental_impls door is deleted. The rename from supplemental_*_target is NOT the fix while selection is still name-match (module_path == \"std.algebra\" && name == \"GroupCompletion\") — see dissolve-on."

data repr_grounding_group_completion_carrier_trigger: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "std.algebra",
    decl_name: "Ring",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

fn repr_grounding_group_completion_carrier(module_path: String, name: String) -> Bool {
  module_path == "std.algebra" && name == "GroupCompletion"
}

fn repr_grounding_supplemental_bool_host_bridge_target(module_path: String, name: String) -> Bool {
  module_path == "std.types" && name == "Bool"
}

data pair_completion_shape_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "Dissolves when v2 derives these rows from the std.algebra Ring/AbelianGroup inhabitance witnesses directly, so the Grothendieck construction is read off the algebra rather than tabulated here; tracked by the numeric-tower grounding lane.")

data pair_completion_uses_rhs_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: std.trait_derive_shape.pair_completion_arm_uses_rhs / pair_completion_body_uses_rhs — variant-discriminating Bool predicates over PairCompletionOperand / PairCompletionBody, retained only because operand arity is DERIVED from the formula rather than stored (storing an arity field would be a second representation of what the arms already say). They dissolve into an arity projection car")

data repr_grounding_bool_host_bridge_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: repr_grounding_supplemental_bool_host_bridge_target / v1_emit_enum_supplemental_impls — Bool↔host-bool bridge door. Dissolves with the Value::Null-split / Bool True|False ↔ Value::Bool grounding lane (DESIGN open thread; gunbc.plans.value_null_split). Kept as the remaining half of the old supplemental door; do not ground the bridge in an emitter cleanup (§3).")

data repr_grounding_group_completion_carrier_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: repr_grounding_group_completion_carrier — name-match module_path/name selection. Dissolves when capability selection for GroupCompletion pair-completion is derived from std.algebra Ring/AbelianGroup inhabitance witnesses (same terminal as pair_completion_shape_dissolve_on / numeric-tower grounding), so the carrier is identified by inhabitance rather than string equality. Terminal trig")

data trait_derive_shape_dissolve_on: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "DISCHARGED BY B1, and recorded rather than deleted because the superseded wording named a type that no longer exists and would misread as pending. It read: ReprGroundingDeriveTrait members that name Rust-specific authorities migrate beside each target's spelling table when a second consumer exists. That migration is done -- the alphabet is re-homed as RustCapability in extdeps.languages.rust.capabilities, and it did NOT wait for a second consumer, because the parameterized shape table (TargetCapabilityShapeTable<K>) removed std's dependency on any carrier instead of relocating it. WHAT REMAINS OWED HERE is a different, smaller fact and is stated so it is not mistaken for the discharged one: this module still names the SOURCE-SHAPE vocabulary (ReprGroundingDeriveElemShape) as a closed coproduct authored by hand, which dissolves when element shape is derived from the declared type's own structure rather than classified by a seed-side match -- the same terminal as pair_completion_shape_dissolve_on.")
